# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-traitforge/blob/279b2887e3d38bc219a05d332cbcb0655b2dc644/contracts/EntropyGenerator/EntropyGenerator.sol#L47-L81


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The entropy initialization functions in EntropyGenerator contract contain a wrong validation logic that allows the value `999999` (referred to as "the Golden God") to appear in the first two initialization batches, while the intended logic is to exclude it from the first two batches.

## Proof of Concept

The project documentation contains the following content:

```md
Entropy is set into uint256 slots, with each slot able to contain a 78 digit number - or 13 concatenated entropies. Thus 770 slots are needed to contain 10,000 entropies. Genesis entropy is seeded using blockhash. Setting all 770 uint256 slots costs 15 million gas, and due to contract and block size limits, this cannot be done in contract initialization. Instead, a function writes in entropy in 3 passes after the contract is deployed. 

There is a certain entropy, “999999” which is referred to as “the Golden God”, since it has perfect parameters and will exceed all other entities if played correctly. The Golden God is scanned for and is kept out of the first 2 passes but is deliberately set in the final pass (at some random point). The Golden God should be the most valuable entity.
```

This means, according to the design logic, that the following first two writeEntropyBatch functions in the EntropyGenerator contract, which initialize entropy values in the first 512 slots, should not contain the value 999999, "the Golden God".

```solidity
// Functions to initialize entropy values in batches to spread gas cost over multiple transactions
  function writeEntropyBatch1() public {
    require(lastInitializedIndex < batchSize1, "Batch 1 already initialized.");

    uint256 endIndex = lastInitializedIndex + batchSize1; // calculate the end index for the batch
    unchecked {
      for (uint256 i = lastInitializedIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {
        uint256 pseudoRandomValue = uint256(
          keccak256(abi.encodePacked(block.number, i))
        ) % uint256(10) ** 78; // generate a pseudo-random value using block number and index
        require(pseudoRandomValue != 999999, "Invalid value, retry.");
        entropySlots[i] = pseudoRandomValue; // store the value in the slots array
      }
    }
    lastInitializedIndex = endIndex;
  }

  // Second batch initialization
  function writeEntropyBatch2() public {
    require(
      lastInitializedIndex >= batchSize1 && lastInitializedIndex < batchSize2,
      "Batch 2 not ready or already initialized."
    );

    uint256 endIndex = lastInitializedIndex + batchSize1;
    unchecked {
      for (uint256 i = lastInitializedIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {
        uint256 pseudoRandomValue = uint256(
          keccak256(abi.encodePacked(block.number, i))
        ) % uint256(10) ** 78;
        require(pseudoRandomValue != 999999, "Invalid value, retry.");
        entropySlots[i] = pseudoRandomValue;
      }
    }
    lastInitializedIndex = endIndex;
  }

  // Allows setting a specific entropy slot with a value
  function writeEntropyBatch3() public {
    require(
      lastInitializedIndex >= batchSize2 && lastInitializedIndex < maxSlotIndex,
      "Batch 3 not ready or already completed."
    );
    unchecked {
      for (uint256 i = lastInitializedIndex; i < maxSlotIndex; i++) {
        uint256 pseudoRandomValue = uint256(
          keccak256(abi.encodePacked(block.number, i))
        ) % uint256(10) ** 78;
        entropySlots[i] = pseudoRandomValue;
      }
    }
    lastInitializedIndex = maxSlotIndex;
  }
```

However, there is a logical error in the code that allows the value `999999` or "the Golden God" to actually appear in the first two passes.

Specifically, the check logic is `require(pseudoRandomValue != 999999, "Invalid value, retry.");`. However, the computed `pseudoRandomValue` is a 78-digit decimal number, so this check is ineffective. For example, if the computed `pseudoRandomValue` is `999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999`, this slot's 13 concatenated entropies would all be "the Golden God", which can still pass the validation, contrary to the expectation.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
A feasible solution is to implement a `checkGoldenGod` function to perform the validation.

```solidity
  function checkGoldenGod(uint256 slotValue) internal view returns (bool) {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < 13; i++) {
      uint256 entropy = (slotValue / (10 ** (72 - (i*6)))) % 1000000;
      require(entropy != 999999, "Invalid value, retry.");
    }
  }

  // Functions to initialize entropy values in batches to spread gas cost over multiple transactions
  function writeEntropyBatch1() public {
    require(lastInitializedIndex < batchSize1, "Batch 1 already initialized.");

    uint256 endIndex = lastInitializedIndex + batchSize1; // calculate the end index for the batch
    unchecked {
      for (uint256 i = lastInitializedIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {
        uint256 pseudoRandomValue = uint256(
          keccak256(abi.encodePacked(block.number, i))
        ) % uint256(10) ** 78; // generate a pseudo-random value using block number and index
        // require(pseudoRandomValue != 999999, "Invalid value, retry.");
        checkGoldenGod(pseudoRandomValue);
        entropySlots[i] = pseudoRandomValue; // store the value in the slots array
      }
    }
    lastInitializedIndex = endIndex;
  }
```

It is important to note that this will increase gas consumption, so it might be necessary to divide the first 512 slots into more batches to be processed separately.





